[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Layer-2 VLAN configuration on the office access switch (switch1). Mechanism: The two desks are no longer in the same broadcast domain, so Windows/SMB discovery (NetBIOS/mDNS/broadcasts) and “seeing each other” fails, even though routed IP connectivity may still allow manual pings.

Localization: On switch1, ports for the nearby laptops are split across two VLAN bridges:
- br_vlan10 members include eth3 and eth4 (where laptop2 172.16.10.12 and laptop3 172.16.10.13 connect).
- br_vlan20 contains eth7 (where laptop0 172.16.20.10 connects).

Evidence:
- laptop2 ↔ laptop3 ping succeeds (same subnet 172.16.10.0/24).
- laptop2/3 ↔ laptop0 ping also succeeds but with TTL 63, indicating inter-VLAN routing, not L2 adjacency.
- switch1 config shows eth3/eth4 in br_vlan10, while eth7 is in br_vlan20 (bridge vlan/show output), proving L2 separation.
- IPs confirm different subnets: laptop2/3 on 172.16.10.0/24 vs laptop0 on 172.16.20.0/24.

Root cause: Port switch1 eth7 (cd_sw1_fa7_1) was moved to VLAN 20, separating that desk from its neighbor(s) on VLAN 10.

Corrective fix: Reassign switch1 port eth7 to the same VLAN/bridge as the adjacent desk(s): remove eth7 from br_vlan20 and add it to br_vlan10 (restore access VLAN 10). After the change, ensure the laptop on eth7 receives/uses an IP in 172.16.10.0/24 (via DHCP or static update) and default gateway 172.16.10.1. This restores same-L2 broadcast visibility and file sharing between the two desks. === END ===